.TH RRCC 1 "@DATE@" "@VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rrcc \- RPM Repository Consistency Checker
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rrcc
[\fIoptions\fR]
.IR path " [" path " ...]"
.br
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rrcc
verifies that a local RPM repository, typically a mirror of a remote
repository, is consistent with its own metadata.
Every package listed in the repository's
.I primary.xml
must exist on disk and have the size recorded in the metadata.
Optionally, the checksum of each package is verified as well and files on disk
that are not referenced by the metadata are reported.
.PP
Every metadata file listed in
.I repodata/repomd.xml
(primary, filelists, other, updateinfo, comps, modules, and their sqlite and
zchunk variants) is checked as well: it must exist and have the size and
checksum recorded in
.IR repomd.xml .
A primary metadata file that does not match is an error, since the package
list cannot be trusted.
.PP
A repository served over HTTP or HTTPS can be checked in the same way by
giving its URL instead of a directory.
The metadata is downloaded to a temporary directory, the presence and size of
each package is checked with a HEAD request, and with
.B \-\-checksum
each package is downloaded and hashed.
.PP
Each
.I path
must be the directory (or URL) that
.B contains
the
.I repodata
directory, that is, the same directory that would be used as a
.I baseurl
in a repository definition.
With
.BR \-\-top\-level ,
each
.I path
is instead treated as a parent directory that is searched recursively for
repositories.
.PP
If
.I repomd.xml
also lists a
.I primary_zck
entry (the zchunk-compressed copy of
.I primary.xml
produced by
.BR "createrepo_c \-\-zck" ),
it is cross-checked against the plain primary metadata.
It must exist, be readable, and list exactly the same packages with the same
sizes and checksums.
A
.I primary_zck
file that cannot be read is reported as a problem.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-top\-level
Treat each
.I path
as a parent directory containing multiple repositories.
For a URL, the directory listings generated by the web server are followed.
Generated index pages that link a directory as
.I dir/index.html
or without a trailing slash are understood too.
Every directory below it that contains
.I repodata/repomd.xml
is found automatically and checked as a separate repository.
This is useful when several release or architecture trees, or
.IR os / debug / source
variants, are mirrored under one parent directory.
.TP
.B \-\-checksum
Also verify the checksum of every package.
This is slow because every RPM file has to be read.
Without this option only the presence and size of each package are checked.
For a repository given as a URL, this also verifies the checksum of every
metadata file, which means downloading all of them.
Without it, only the metadata files that
.B rrcc
reads anyway have their checksum verified, and the others are checked for
presence and size.
.TP
.B \-\-extra
Also report
.I *.rpm
files found on disk that are not referenced by
.IR primary.xml ,
such as orphaned or stale files.
For a URL this requires that the web server has directory listings enabled.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-symlinks\-outside
For a repository in a directory, follow a symlink that leads outside of it,
instead of refusing it like any other location outside of the repository.
Only use this for a mirror made in a way you control, for example one that
symlinks packages in from a shared pool: a mirror from someone else could
otherwise use a symlink to make
.B rrcc
read, and report the size or a checksum match of, an arbitrary file.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-age " DAYS"
Report a repository whose
.I repomd.xml
is more than
.I DAYS
days old as a problem.
The age is taken from the newest
.I <timestamp>
of the entries in
.IR repomd.xml ,
which is when the metadata was generated, not from the modification time of
the file.
.I DAYS
can be a fraction, such as 0.5 for twelve hours.
A repository without any timestamp is reported as a problem too.
This catches a mirror that is consistent but is no longer being synced.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-newest\-only
Only check the latest version of each package listed in
.IR primary.xml ,
and skip the older ones.
This matches
.B dnf reposync \-\-newest\-only
and is meant for checking a mirror made that way.
The latest version is picked per package name and architecture.
For repositories with modules (modular repositories) the same rules as in
dnf 4, the
.BR dnf (8)
of RHEL 8 to 10, are used: the latest version of each non-modular package, all packages of the
newest build of each module stream, and all packages of any older build of a
stream that contains the latest version of one of its packages.
The module metadata is then read from
.IR modules.yaml .
Skipped packages are still counted as part of the repository, so they are not
reported by
.B \-\-extra
and are still included in the
.I primary_zck
cross-check.
.TP
.B \-\-ignore\-modules
With
.BR \-\-newest\-only ,
ignore the module metadata and keep just the latest version of each package,
modular or not.
This is what the reposync of dnf 5 (Fedora 41 and newer) does, so use it to
check a mirror of a modular repository made with dnf 5.
For a repository without modules it makes no difference.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-jobs " \fIN\fR"
Check
.I N
packages in parallel for a repository given as a URL (default 8, 1 disables
parallelism).
Each worker keeps its own connection to the server alive, so this is also the
number of simultaneous connections.
Ignored for repositories in directories.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print only the repositories that have problems or could not be read, without
the notes and the summary, and nothing at all if every repository is
consistent.
Meant for
.BR cron (8),
which then only sends mail when something is wrong.
The exit status is the same as without this option.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print a line for every package checked.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-progress
Don't show the progress line.
When standard error is a terminal, and neither
.B \-q
nor
.B \-v
is given, a line on it shows which repository is being checked and what is
being done, and while files are checked a bar with their number, the
bytes hashed (with
.BR \-\-checksum ),
the speed and the time left.
It only appears once a repository has taken more than half a second, and is
cleared before the report of the repository is printed.
It uses colors unless
.B NO_COLOR
is set or
.B TERM
is
.IR dumb ,
and plain ASCII unless the terminal's encoding is UTF-8.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-h
Show a short help, with a one-line description of each option, and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show the full help, with a longer description of each option, and exit.
.SS "TLS options"
These options only apply to repositories given as
.I https://
URLs.
They correspond to the
.BR sslcacert ,
.BR sslclientcert ,
.B sslclientkey
and
.B sslverify
settings of a repository in
.BR dnf.conf (5).
.TP
.BI \-\-ca\-cert " FILE"
Trust only the CA certificates in the PEM file
.IR FILE ,
instead of the CA certificates of the system.
.I FILE
can also be a directory of CA certificates prepared with
.BR "openssl rehash" .
.TP
.BI \-\-client\-cert " FILE"
Authenticate to the server with the client certificate in the PEM file
.IR FILE .
The file can also contain the private key.
It is only ever sent to the host given on the command line, never to a
different host reached through a redirect.
.TP
.BI \-\-client\-key " FILE"
The private key for
.BR \-\-client\-cert ,
if it is in a separate PEM file.
Encrypted private keys are not supported.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-insecure
Do not verify the certificate of the server.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-strict\-x509
Accept a server certificate that does not follow RFC 5280 strictly.
Since Python 3.13, certificates are verified in strict mode by default, which
rejects many certificates from home-made CAs, for example with the error
.IR "Missing Authority Key Identifier" ,
even though
.BR curl (1)
and
.BR dnf (8)
accept them.
Unlike
.BR \-\-insecure ,
the certificate and the host name are still verified.
With older Python versions this option changes nothing.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR http_proxy ", " https_proxy
The proxy to use for repositories given as
.I http://
or
.I https://
URLs, in the form
.IR http://host:port ,
optionally with basic authentication as
.IR http://user:password@host:port .
The upper-case forms
.B HTTP_PROXY
and
.B HTTPS_PROXY
are also recognized.
Only
.I http://
proxies are supported; HTTPS repositories are reached through a tunnel
.RB ( CONNECT )
in the proxy.
.TP
.B no_proxy
Comma-separated list of host names, domains or addresses that are contacted
directly instead of through the proxy.
.TP
.BR NO_COLOR ", " FORCE_COLOR ", " PYTHON_COLORS ", " TERM
Whether the progress line uses colors, as for Python 3.13 and newer:
.B PYTHON_COLORS
set to 1 or 0 turns them on or off, else a non-empty
.B NO_COLOR
turns them off and a non-empty
.B FORCE_COLOR
on, and without these they are used unless
.B TERM
is unset or
.IR dumb .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Everything checked is consistent.
.TP
.B 1
At least one problem was found in at least one repository.
.TP
.B 2
The metadata could not be read for one or more repositories, or no
repositories were found under a
.B \-\-top\-level
path.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.B rrcc
runs on Python 3.6 or newer and uses only the Python standard library, except
for the following optional cases that depend on the compression format of the
repository metadata:
.TP
.I .gz " and " .xz
Always handled by the Python standard library.
.TP
.I .zst
Requires Python 3.14 or newer (standard library module
.IR compression.zstd )
or the third-party
.I zstandard
Python module (package
.IR python3\-zstandard ).
.TP
.I .zck
Requires the external
.B unzck
command from the
.I zchunk
package, which must be in
.IR PATH .
This works on any Python version.
On RHEL and derivatives the package is in EPEL.
.SH SECURITY
The metadata of the repository, and the server it comes from, are treated as
untrusted input.
Locations in the metadata that are absolute, or that lead outside of the
repository with
.IR .. ,
are refused and reported as problems, and are never opened or requested.
Redirects are followed, but not from
.I https://
to
.IR http:// ,
and not to anything but
.I http://
and
.IR https:// .
What is downloaded is bounded by the sizes in the metadata, and names from the
repository are printed with control characters escaped.
.PP
.B rrcc
checks that a repository is consistent, not that it is authentic: it does not
verify GPG signatures (of
.I repomd.xml
or of the packages), and it accepts the checksum algorithms that the metadata
uses, including MD5 and SHA-1.
Use
.B \-\-insecure
only for servers that you trust in some other way.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check a single repository for presence and size of all packages:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rrcc /srv/mirror/rocky/9/BaseOS/x86_64/os
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check every repository below a mirror directory, verifying checksums and
reporting orphaned files:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rrcc \-\-top\-level \-\-checksum \-\-extra /srv/mirror
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check a remote repository over HTTP:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rrcc \-\-jobs 16 http://mirror.example.com/pub/epel/8/Modular/x86_64
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check a repository on the Red Hat CDN with the entitlement certificate of a
registered system:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rrcc \-\-ca\-cert /etc/rhsm/ca/redhat\-uep.pem \\
     \-\-client\-cert /etc/pki/entitlement/\fISERIAL\fR.pem \\
     \-\-client\-key /etc/pki/entitlement/\fISERIAL\fR\-key.pem \\
     https://cdn.redhat.com/content/dist/rhel9/9/x86_64/baseos/os
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use in a script after a mirror run:
.PP
.RS
.nf
reposync ... && rrcc /srv/mirror/myrepo || echo "mirror is broken"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check from a separate monitoring job that a mirror synced within the last two
days:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rrcc \-\-max\-age 2 /srv/mirror/myrepo
.fi
.RE
.SH HOMEPAGE
The source code, issue tracker and latest release of
.B rrcc
are available at
.UR https://github.com/johanhedin/rrcc
.UE .
.SH AUTHOR
Johan Hedin
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR createrepo_c (8),
.BR dnf (8),
.BR reposync (1),
.BR unzck (1)
